gs published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Maine enters the 2028 presidential race as an open contest, with the incumbent term-limited and neither party's nominee settled. The state's partisan lean favors Democrats by about four points, and no general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing have been published, so fundamentals carry the forecast. Recent coverage centers on the Democratic primary and an intraparty fight over ranked-choice voting rather than the general election itself.\n\nOn the current evidence, Democrats hold a modest edge rooted in the state's lean, paired with wide uncertainty given the absence of polling. A national environment breaking toward either party, or the profiles of the eventual nominees, could move Maine considerably. The state also splits its electoral votes by congressional district, keeping Republican paths to a partial haul alive even in a Democratic-leaning state.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.679, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6494, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.645, "margin": 4.0}]}